.TH TNT-EXEC 7 "2026-08-18" "TNT 1.3.0"
.SH NAME
tnt-exec \- TNT SSH exec command and output protocol
.SH DESCRIPTION
TNT accepts non-interactive commands through an SSH exec request.
The same command set is exposed by
.BR tntctl (1).
The maximum exec command string is 1023 bytes.
.P
Command output is written to the SSH channel.
The server then sends an SSH exit status and closes the channel.
Human-readable help and diagnostics may be localized and are not a parsing
interface.
.SH INVOCATION
With a standard SSH client:
.P
.EX
ssh \-p 2222 user@chat.example.com health
ssh \-p 2222 user@chat.example.com stats \-\-json
ssh \-p 2222 user@chat.example.com "tail \-n 20"
.EE
.P
With the TNT wrapper:
.P
.EX
tntctl chat.example.com health
tntctl chat.example.com stats \-\-json
tntctl chat.example.com tail \-n 20
.EE
.SH COMMANDS
.SS help
.SY help
.YS
.SY \-\-help
.YS
Print a localized command summary for people.
The wording and layout are not stable for parsers.
.SS health
.SY health
.YS
Write the following line:
.P
.EX
ok
.EE
.SS users
.SY users
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.YS
Without
.BR \-\-json ,
write one active nickname per line.
With it, write one JSON array of strings followed by a newline.
An empty room is represented as
.BR [] .
.SS stats
.SY stats
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.YS
Without
.BR \-\-json ,
write one space-separated key and value per line:
.P
.EX
status ok
online_users 0
message_count 0
client_capacity 64
active_connections 1
uptime_seconds 12
.EE
.P
With
.BR \-\-json ,
write one JSON object followed by a newline.
The object has these members:
.TP
.B status
String value
.BR ok .
.TP
.B online_users
Integer count of interactive users in the room.
.TP
.B message_count
Integer count of messages in the live in-memory history.
.TP
.B client_capacity
Integer room client capacity.
.TP
.B active_connections
Integer count of admitted SSH connections.
.TP
.B uptime_seconds
Integer server uptime in seconds.
.SS tail
.SY tail
.RI [ count ]
.YS
.SY tail
.B \-n
.I count
.YS
Write recent live in-memory messages in chronological order.
The default
.I count
is 20; the valid range is 1 through 100.
.P
Each message is one tab-separated line:
.P
.EX
2026-05-25T12:00:00Z<TAB>alice<TAB>hello
.EE
.P
The three fields are an RFC 3339 UTC timestamp, sender, and content.
.B tail
does not read the complete persistent log.
.SS dump
.SY dump
.RI [ count ]
.YS
.SY dump
.B \-n
.I count
.YS
.SY dump
.B \-\-all
.YS
Write valid records from the active persistent message log in chronological
order.
The output is the exact format defined by
.BR tnt\-message\-log (5).
.P
Without an argument,
.B dump
writes the last 100 valid records.
The valid
.I count
range is 1 through 10000.
.B \-\-all
writes every valid record in the active log.
Malformed records are skipped.
A missing log produces no output and succeeds.
.SS post
.SY post
.I message
.YS
The complete exec request,
including the command name and separating whitespace,
must fit the 1023-byte exec limit.
The server removes leading and trailing whitespace from
.IR message .
The result must be nonempty valid UTF-8.
It cannot contain a C0 or C1 control character or DEL.
The final stored content must contain at most 1023 bytes.
On success,
.B post
writes:
.P
.EX
posted
.EE
.P
The sender is the SSH login name when that value is a valid TNT nickname;
otherwise it is
.BR anonymous .
The displayed name is limited to 20 Unicode characters.
.P
A message beginning with
.B "/me "
creates an action.
The stored content consists of the sender, one space, and the action text,
and must fit the same stored-content limit.
.SS exit
.SY exit
.YS
Produce no output and succeed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime, allocation, I/O, or persistence operation failed.
.B post
also returns 1 for invalid UTF-8 or a prohibited control character.
.TP
.B 64
The command name, argument shape, count, or message length was invalid.
.P
.BR tntctl (1)
uses additional local statuses for SSH transport and wrapper failures.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The documented command names, argument forms, exit statuses, and JSON
member
names and scalar types are stable throughout TNT 1.x.
.P
A minor release may append text statistics keys or JSON object members.
Consumers must ignore keys and object members they do not recognize.
No such extension changes the type of an existing JSON member.
.P
Text intended for people, including
.B help
and error wording, is not stable.
.SH CAVEATS
In anonymous-access mode, the SSH login name used by
.B post
is not authenticated.
A shared access token authenticates possession of the token.
It does not establish ownership of
the login name.
.P
.B tail
reads the bounded live buffer, whereas
.B dump
reads the active on-disk log.
Their results can differ during rotation or after invalid persisted records
have been skipped.
.SH EXAMPLES
List users as JSON:
.P
.EX
ssh \-p 2222 chat.example.com users \-\-json
.EE
.P
Export every valid active-log record:
.P
.EX
tntctl chat.example.com dump \-\-all
.EE
.P
Post an action:
.P
.EX
tntctl \-l operator chat.example.com post "/me deploys v2"
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR tntctl (1),
.BR tnt\-message\-log (5),
.BR tnt\-chat (7),
.BR tnt (8)
